---
description: A column asked for a column type that gridOptions.columnTypes does not define.
---

## Column Type Not Defined

A column asked for the type {% errorParam name="type" fallback="currency" /%} and the grid found no definition under that name, so it was skipped. The column keeps its other properties, and any other types on it still apply.

Column types are declared once in the `columnTypes` grid option and referenced by name from a column:

```js
const currencyFormatter = (params) => (params.value == null ? '' : `£${params.value.toLocaleString()}`);
```

```{% frameworkTransform=true spaceBetweenProperties=true %}
const gridOptions = {
    columnTypes: {
        currency: {
            width: 150,
            valueFormatter: currencyFormatter,
        },
    },
    columnDefs: [{ field: 'boughtPrice', type: 'currency' }],
}
```

See [Column Types](./column-definitions/#column-types).

### Check the name

Each name is looked up on its own, so one wrong name does not stop the rest working — which is why the grid can name a type when the definition looks present:

- `type` accepts one name, several as a comma-separated string, or an array. `type: 'currency, shaded'` and `type: ['currency', 'shaded']` are equivalent, and each is resolved separately.
- Only `numericColumn` and `rightAligned` are [provided by the grid](./column-definitions/#provided-column-types). Every other name has to be in `columnTypes`.
- Cell data types are a different mechanism. `'number'`, `'date'`, `'text'` and the rest belong to `cellDataType`, not `type`. See [Cell Data Types](./cell-data-types/).

### Check where the type came from

The type need not be written on the column the message names. The grid takes the first of these it finds:

1. `type` on the column definition.
2. The `columnTypes` of a [cell data type definition](./cell-data-types/#providing-custom-cell-data-types), applied to every column using that data type.
3. `type` on the [`defaultColDef`](./column-definitions/#default-column-definitions), applied to every column.

So a type named on `defaultColDef` or a custom data type reports against columns you never set a type on, once for each of them.

### Check `columnTypes` is a grid option

`columnTypes` sits alongside `columnDefs` at the grid level, not inside a column definition. Declaring it on a column, or on `defaultColDef`, leaves the grid with no definitions to resolve against.

{% note %}
A column type cannot set `type` itself ([error #35](./errors/35)), and cannot reuse a provided name such as `numericColumn` ([error #34](./errors/34)).
{% /note %}
